{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the problem, we start by noting that six points are equally spaced around a circle of radius 1. This means the points divide the circle into six equal arcs, each subtending an angle of \\(60^\\circ\\) at the center of the circle. Let's label the points as \\(A, B, C, D, E, F\\) in order around the circle.\n\nWe need to find a triangle formed by three of these points that is neither equilateral nor isosceles. The possible side lengths of the triangle are determined by the distances between the points. The distances are:\n- \\(1\\) (distance between two adjacent points)\n- \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) (distance between points that are two apart, i.e., skipping one point)\n- \\(2\\) (distance between points that are three apart, i.e., opposite each other)\n\nThe only combination of side lengths that form a triangle and is neither equilateral nor isosceles is \\(1, \\sqrt{3}, 2\\). This is because:\n- A triangle with all sides equal (equilateral) would have side lengths of 1, 1, 1 or \\(\\sqrt{3}\\), \\(\\sqrt{3}\\), \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) or 2, 2, 2, none of which fit.\n- A triangle with two sides equal (isosceles) would have side lengths of 1, 1, \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) or 1, 1, 2 or \\(\\sqrt{3}\\), \\(\\sqrt{3}\\), 2, none of which fit.\n\n","prefix_end":1197,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,3491,11,582,1191,553,26305,429,4743,3501,525,18308,63828,2163,264,12671,315,10578,220,16,13,1096,3363,279,3501,21749,279,12671,1119,4743,6144,66750,11,1817,41029,2459,458,9210,315,17767,21,15,24884,43298,57758,518,279,4126,315,279,12671,13,6771,594,2383,279,3501,438,17767,32,11,425,11,356,11,422,11,468,11,434,57758,304,1973,2163,279,12671,382,1654,1184,311,1477,264,21495,14122,553,2326,315,1493,3501,429,374,13866,3232,42939,6329,374,436,346,642,13,576,3204,3108,28316,315,279,21495,525,10838,553,279,26552,1948,279,3501,13,576,2